my kids love their toys  there's the ice cream where's the ice  cream and most of the time i love that  they love their toys especially when i'm  working from home but here's some bad  news toy departments across the country  may have some empty shelves this fall  and the toys that we will be able to buy  they're about to get more expensive in  some cases we're talking double digit  increases yeah you've heard a lot about  inflation which was already prompting  toy companies to raise their prices but  you may not have heard about a small  coronavirus outbreak a few months ago  over in china that might be pushing  those toy prices even higher  that one outbreak has caused ripple  effects across the entire 95 billion  dollar toy industry it feels way too  early to think about the holidays but  seriously should we all be stockpiling  toys now to fill those stockings as a  reporter for the wall street journal who  obviously has some skin in the game here  i decided to dig into the data to find  out what happened and how it's going to  affect all of us toy consumers  [Music]  here in the us three billion toys are  sold every single year and according to  the toy association approximately 85  percent of those toys are produced over  in china some in the industry even joke  that china is the new north pole and  while there are factories all over china  guangdong province in southern china is  considered the toy capital of the world  since ocean freight is much cheaper than  air most of those toys travel stateside  by boat not plane but just like an  entire highway gets jammed when one car  breaks down  shipping lanes get backed up when  something goes wrong at a port  and that's what happened back in may  when some dock workers and others at the  yantian port in guangdong province  tested positive for kovit 19. yantian is  not as well known as other major ports  but it's so big that it moved 50 more  cargo than the port of la last year  which is the busiest port in america  even though there were just over a dozen  coronavirus cases chinese authorities  came down hard and shut down parts of  the province operations at the port fell  to 30 capacity and hundreds of thousands  of containers piled up on the docks with  ships unable to pick them up as you can  see in the satellite image because goods  simply weren't moving some factories  even shut down it took nearly a month  for the port to resume full operations  but the impact is far from over the  backup has affected all kinds of  companies and another coronavirus  cluster up near shanghai is having a  similar effect on another huge port but  for toy companies the outbreak at  yantian has been particularly painful  that's because toys are shipped out of  china in the summer in order to hit us  shelves in time for the holidays with so  many businesses desperate to move  product shipping prices which were  already high soared to new records a  forty foot container that cost about  seven thousand dollars to ship in  mid-may rose to nine thousand dollars by  mid-june and prices continued to climb  from there with container space tight  some toy makers even engaged in bidding  wars just to move their product that  especially hurts small businesses which  don't have the big shipping contracts or  clout that say a walmart or a target  does  one business owner told me he just paid  more than 24 thousand dollars to get  product off the docks at yantian stuff  that was supposed to ship a month ago  even major toy makers which have the  ability to make products in different  countries and use various shipping lanes  have been talking about how crazy ocean  freight costs are in their earnings  calls experts i talk to don't expect the  shipping issues or costs to get ironed  out before the end of the year and that  means toy companies are probably gonna  have to pick and choose what gets  shipped  that ultimately means fewer toy options  for the holidays and higher prices for  the toys that do make it onto shelves  toy giants like hasbro and mattel were  already planning price hikes due to the  rising cost of raw material and labor  and analysts i talked to say that these  shipping backlogs are likely to push  those hikes even higher and what about  smaller companies some say they simply  won't be able to survive if they don't  increase their prices and unfortunately  analysts say that once toy prices go up  it's very unlikely they'll ever come  back down so if toy prices are going up  should we all go stockpile toys right  now well not if you want that shiny new  stuff that'll be on shelves this fall  but the question is will we all be  willing to pay more for toys  based on recent trends it looks like the  answer to that question is yes toy sales  are better than ever and they're  expected to remain strong through the  end of the year  but you're the one buying toys so you  tell me let me know by leaving a comment  below  and if you want to know more about the  news events that are impacting our world  subscribe to the channel for more videos  like this one  see you next time  [Music]
 

